Subject: mtd: rawnand: ids: add Hynix H27UBG8T2C by full ID

The H27UBG8T2C (Hynix 20nm, 32Gbit MLC, ID ad d7 94 91 60 44) has 8KiB
pages with 640 bytes of OOB in 2MiB erase blocks of 256 pages. Nothing in
the ID tables matches it by full ID, so it falls through to the legacy
0xd7 entry and hynix_nand_decode_id(), which reads byte 3 as a 4KiB page
with 128 bytes of OOB in 512-page blocks.

Every number in that is checkable from the code. Fed the six ID bytes,
the non-JEDEC path of hynix_nand_decode_id() and its two helpers produce
page 4096 (2048 << (0x91 & 3)), erase 2MiB (bit 7 set, (0x91 >> 4) & 3 ==
1), OOB 128 (oobsize code 0) and an ECC requirement of 32b/1024B (level 6,
technology 4, so 24 + 8 * (6 - 5)). The first field report of this part on
a Meson8b board -- a Xunlei WS1508 -- printed exactly those four values:

  nand: 4096 MiB, MLC, erase size: 2048 KiB, page size: 4096, OOB size: 128
  nand: WARNING: d0048600.nand-controller: the ECC used on your system
        (8b/1024B) is too weak compared to the one required by the NAND
        chip (32b/1024B)

The 8KiB/640 geometry is the one Amlogic's own NAND stack uses for this
ID, which is what the box's factory firmware ran on: drivers/amlnf/phy/
id_table.c in the vendor u-boot lists it as

  {"C revision 20nm NAND 4GiB H27UBG8T2C",
   {NAND_MFR_HYNIX, 0xd7, 0x94, 0x91, 0x60, 0x44},
   8192, 4096, 0x200000, 640, ...}

with the same 8192/640 in the older drivers/mtd/nand/aml_nand.c. The A
(ad d7 94 9a 74 42) and B (ad d7 94 da 74 c3) revisions next to it in
both tables already decode to 8KiB pages here and need no entry. The
internal inconsistency of the mis-decode is a second witness: 32 bits of
correction per 1KiB needs about 56 bytes of parity per step, i.e. 224 per
4KiB page, which a 128-byte OOB cannot hold.

The mis-decode is not cosmetic. With page_shift one too small, nand_base
addresses erase block E as row E << 9, which the chip -- 256 pages per
block -- reads as block 2E. Blocks below the middle of what the core
believes is a 2048-block device therefore land on the even-numbered
blocks of the whole chip, and from block 1024 upwards the row exceeds
the chip's address space altogether. On the WS1508 report an ubiformat
of the upper partition ran on into that range, and both erase failures
it hit lie inside it, at blocks 1033 and 1046. A driver-side write guard
that reasons in the decoded geometry cannot see any of this.

The ECC requirement keeps the 32b/1024B the ID's own ECC field encodes
under the decode nand_hynix.c already applies, which is also what
Amlogic's older driver chose for this part (NAND_ECC_BCH16_MODE, 16 bits
per 512 bytes). Scrambling is required, as the decode would also have
set from the technology field.

diff --git a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/nand_ids.c b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/nand_ids.c
index 650351c..02367db 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/nand_ids.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/nand_ids.c
@@ -50,6 +50,10 @@ struct nand_flash_dev nand_flash_ids[] = {
 	{"SDTNRGAMA 64G 3.3V 8-bit",
 		{ .id = {0x45, 0xde, 0x94, 0x93, 0x76, 0x50} },
 		  SZ_16K, SZ_8K, SZ_4M, 0, 6, 1280, NAND_ECC_INFO(40, SZ_1K) },
+	{"H27UBG8T2CTR-BC 32G 3.3V 8-bit",
+		{ .id = {0xad, 0xd7, 0x94, 0x91, 0x60, 0x44} },
+		  SZ_8K, SZ_4K, SZ_2M, NAND_NEED_SCRAMBLING, 6, 640,
+		  NAND_ECC_INFO(32, SZ_1K) },
 	{"H27UCG8T2ATR-BC 64G 3.3V 8-bit",
 		{ .id = {0xad, 0xde, 0x94, 0xda, 0x74, 0xc4} },
 		  SZ_8K, SZ_8K, SZ_2M, NAND_NEED_SCRAMBLING, 6, 640,
